Minimal docker container that acts as a microsandbox --- a
tiny fish in a tiny can if you will, kind of like sardines
--- specifically tailored towards keeping agents out of
unwanted credential usage while being flexible and
extensible to the point of being dangerous if you want to.
Who am I to say you can't shoot yourself in the foot? I'm
not your mother.

It is hardcoded to work in a LiteLLM setup, where the API
key is served through a proxy inside the sandbox. This part
is currently not flexible at all.

The image has a persistent folder for things like installing
harnesses, npm versions, and pip caches etc. Just think of
it as a super small Linux installation that you can use as
normal.

=== QUICK START ===

Set environment variables on host machine (or preferably
resolve using keychain rather than setting them in bashrc)

    SANDBOX_PROJECT_FOLDER
        denotes the working directory, where the sandbox is
        started. Preferably set this to the folder
        containing coding projects.
    GATEWAY_URL
        denotes the LiteLLM endpoint URL
    GATEWAY_KEY
        denotes the LiteLLM API key

Go into docker compose and configure as seen fit. Since this
is not published on Docker hub yet, you have to build it:

    docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.dev.yaml up -d --build

Then enter the sandbox using docker exec:

    docker exec -it sardine bash

Install whatever harness you want inside the sandbox as you
normally would. This will persist after reboots etc. See
harnesses config below.

To make it a bit less cumbersome and easier to use, you can
create aliases to get you directly into a harness or tool:

    alias pi="docker exec -it sardine pi"

=== BUILDING ===

docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.dev.yaml up -d --build

+===========+
| HARNESSES |
+===========+

=== PI ===

Install pi as normal from https://pi.dev/ inside the sandbox

If you have a pre-existing pi setup it should work as normal
after that, using the already existing local config.

To use it with LiteLLM, first install the extension:

    pi install npm:pi-provider-litellm

Then make sure ~/.pi/agent/models.json looks something like this:

{
  "providers": {},
  "litellm": {
    "providers": {
      "litellm": {
        "baseUrl": "http://gateway:8080/litellm",
        "apiKey": "$LITELLM_API_KEY",
        "allowInsecureHttp": true,
        "enableMcp": true
      }
    }
  }
}
